<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Equatorial Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1910 CE – 1958 CE · capital Brazzaville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The federation of France's colonies in central Africa, governed from Brazzaville, and the first part of the empire to rally to de Gaulle.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> grouped its central African territories into a federation modelled on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ubangi-shari">Ubangi-Shari</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> separated as a colony in 1920. A governor-general ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>, while each colony kept a lieutenant-governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The federation was the poorest part of the French empire. Concession companies, compulsory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> growing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> extraction and forced labour, notably for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-ocean-railway">Congo-Ocean railway</a>, provoked revolts such as the Kongo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wara">Wara</a> rising in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ubangi-shari">Ubangi-Shari</a>. In August 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-eboue">Félix Éboué</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and later governor-general, led the federation to join <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-france">Free France</a>, and the 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville-conference">Brazzaville Conference</a> promised reforms.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1946 its peoples became French citizens and elected deputies. In 1958 the four territories became autonomous republics within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> and the federation was dissolved, its members becoming independent in August 1960.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-equatorial-africa.jpg" alt="French Equatorial Africa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: ComandanteBruh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Equatorial_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1910 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brazzaville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Félix Éboué</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Sango, Lingala, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, <a href="/countries/gabon">Gabon</a>, <a href="/countries/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/chad">Chad</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Afrique-Equatoriale francaise, AEF</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=french-equatorial-africa" aria-label="French Equatorial Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5104 2337 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Equatorial Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5424 2593L5571 1919M5424 2593L5054 728M5424 2593L5262 2418M5424 2593L4997 651M5424 2593L5444 2471M5424 2593L5262 2418M5424 2593L5515 2270M5424 2593L5401 2038"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5571 1919h0M5054 728h0M5262 2418h0M4997 651h0M5444 2471h0M5262 2418h0M5515 2270h0M5401 2038h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5424 2593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5424 2593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Brazzaville. <a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=french-equatorial-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
